.TH ENVS 1 "" "envs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
envs \- context-aware local environment router for the worktree era
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBenvs\fR \fIenv_name\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR ...]
.br
\fBenvs-source-activate\fR [\fIname\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBenvs\fR picks the right \fI.env\fR file based on the current context
(which repo, which branch, which directory), exports its variables, and
runs the given command with them injected. Env files live in one global
place (\fI~/.config/envs/\fR) and a single routing table selects the file
for the current context, so no \fB.env\fR ever has to live inside a
worktree.
.PP
There is no per-project setup: a new worktree or a temporary agent
directory is already routed, and secrets never risk being committed.
.SH USAGE
.TP
\fBenvs\fR \fIenv_name\fR \fIcommand\fR ...
Resolve the routing rule for \fIenv_name\fR in the current directory,
export the matched \fI.env\fR file, and run \fIcommand\fR with it.
.SH CONFIG SYNTAX
The routing table is \fI~/.config/envs/config\fR. Each rule is one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<cond1,cond2,...>env_name:path
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 4
\fB<...>\fR \(em zero or more comma-separated conditions; all must match (AND).
.IP \(bu 4
\fBenv_name\fR \(em the first CLI argument to \fBenvs\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBpath\fR \(em env file location; may contain \fB<keyword>\fR placeholders
that interpolate the runtime context.
.IP \(bu 4
First match wins. Lines starting with \fB#\fR and blank lines are ignored.
.SH CONTEXT KEYWORDS
Usable both as conditions (\fB<key:value>\fR) and as path interpolation
(\fB<key>\fR).
.TP
.B repo
basename of the git remote origin URL, with \fB.git\fR stripped.
.TP
.B org
the org/user segment of that URL.
.TP
.B branch
the current git branch.
.TP
.B current_dir
as a condition, matched against the absolute cwd path with three glob
forms: \fBfoo\fR (substring), \fB*/foo\fR (last segment), and \fB*/foo/*\fR
(interior segment). As path interpolation, the basename of the cwd.
.TP
.B name
the env_name CLI argument; usable in path interpolation only.
.PP
Outside a git repo, \fBrepo\fR, \fBorg\fR and \fBbranch\fR are empty, so
rules using them never match. \fBcurrent_dir\fR always works.
.SH "ENVS-SOURCE (VIRTUALENV STYLE)"
For env vars that persist across multiple commands in one shell, source
\fIenvs-source.sh\fR (or \fIenvs-source.fish\fR) and use:
.TP
\fBenvs-source-activate\fR [\fIname\fR]
Activate source mode; a precmd hook re-evaluates routing every prompt and
swaps the exported keys as you \fBcd\fR.
.TP
.B envs-source-deactivate
Deactivate and restore the original value of every injected key.
.TP
.B envs-source-status
Print current state \(em \fBinactive\fR, or \fBactive\fR with name, matched
file, and injected keys.
.PP
A dev pair (\fIenvs-dev-source.sh\fR, \fBenvs-dev-source-*\fR) uses a
separate variable namespace so both can coexist.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
.nf
# Route "development" inside the myapp repo.
echo '<repo:myapp>development:./myapp/.env.development' >> ~/.config/envs/config
cd ~/code/myapp
envs development pnpm dev
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/envs/config
The routing table.
.TP
.I ~/.config/envs/<project>/.env.<name>
Your env files (secrets).
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/envs
The installed binary.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/envs/
Wrapper scripts, example config, and the full README.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
Full documentation: \fI~/.local/share/envs/README.md\fR and
<https://github.com/fronterior/envs>.
